description:
crates/apiserver/src/handlers/pods.rs:6652 (bind_pod) unconditionally does:

    obj.body["spec"]["nodeName"] = serde_json::Value::String(node_name);

with NO check for whether spec.nodeName is already set. Confirmed against real upstream source (pkg/registry/core/pod/storage/storage.go:238-240, release-1.36):

    if pod.Spec.NodeName != "" {
        return nil, fmt.Errorf("pod %v is already assigned to node %q", pod.Name, pod.Spec.NodeName)
    }

Real kube-apiserver explicitly rejects a second bind on an already-assigned pod (surfaces as 409 Conflict via assignPod's error wrapping). u7s has no equivalent guard anywhere in bind_pod -- confirmed by reading the full handler, no such check exists.

Discovered as a consequence of investigating mayor-4526p (scheduler re-issues POST .../binding for every pod roughly every 30 minutes, globally, for the life of the process, including already-running pods -- e.g. confirmed on kube-system/coredns and the sonobuoy-e2e-job pod itself, both re-bound multiple times while already Running). That bug currently 'gets away with it' only because each re-bind happens to target the SAME node the pod is already on. But nothing in bind_pod prevents a DIFFERENT node from being selected on a later call -- and this became MORE likely, not less, after mayor-pxm9c's fix made node selection load-sensitive (least-loaded) rather than static: node load naturally shifts over the ~30 minutes between a pod's original bind and any subsequent stray re-bind call, so a later call choosing a different node is a live possibility, not a hypothetical.

If that happens: the apiserver would silently overwrite spec.nodeName to the new node while the pod's actual containers keep running on the original node under the original kubelet. The new node's kubelet would observe an assignment with no matching running pod and attempt to start it fresh (image pull, new sandbox) -- while the original kubelet, on seeing spec.nodeName no longer matches its own identity, may tear down its running instance. Net effect: a running pod -- including a restartPolicy=Never Job pod that must never be silently restarted or duplicated -- could be involuntarily migrated, duplicated, or killed, entirely outside any user-visible action.

Fix: bind_pod must check the stored pod's CURRENT spec.nodeName before writing -- if already non-empty (regardless of value), reject with a Conflict/409 matching upstream's 'already assigned to node' semantics, not silently overwrite. This closes the safety gap independent of whatever is currently triggering duplicate bind calls (mayor-4526p is the currently-known trigger, but this guard should exist regardless of trigger, since a race between two legitimate scheduling attempts, a client retry, or any future bug could hit the same unguarded path).

Regression test: bind_pod called twice for the same pod (first bind succeeds, spec.nodeName set), second call with a DIFFERENT target node must be rejected (Conflict), and the pod's spec.nodeName must remain the ORIGINAL value afterward -- this is the test that would have caught this gap and must fail if the guard is reverted.

notes:
Found live while verifying mayor-2mtvs (CR orphan-delete cascade-order fix): creating a namespaced CR (via create_cr_namespaced, cr.rs) with metadata.ownerReferences in the request body silently drops them — the stored object has no ownerReferences at all, even though the identical request against the cluster-scoped create_cr path (no such bug) preserves them correctly (there is an existing regression test create_cr_via_api_preserves_owner_references for the cluster-scoped path only).

Root cause: create_cr_namespaced does an EXTRA ObjectMeta round-trip (cr.rs, right before stamp_cr_fields is called) solely to set metadata.namespace:
    let mut meta: crate::types::ObjectMeta = serde_json::from_value(obj["metadata"].take()).unwrap_or_default();
    meta.namespace = Some(ns.clone());
    obj["metadata"] = serde_json::to_value(meta).unwrap_or_default();
    stamp_cr_fields(&mut obj, &group, &version, &ctx.kind);
ObjectMeta's serde impl only knows declared fields (ownerReferences is a fixed field it DOES declare, actually — need to double-check whether ObjectMeta itself has an ownerReferences field or not; live repro showed it disappears, so either ObjectMeta lacks the field or something else strips it). stamp_cr_fields has an explicit save-before/restore-after specifically because IT does this same round-trip and learned the hard way (see its comment) — but the namespace-setting round-trip above it does not, so by the time stamp_cr_fields runs, ownerReferences is already gone and its own save is a no-op.

Impact: any namespaced CR created with an explicit ownerReference (kubectl create/apply of a CR-owns-CR dependent, or any controller doing so) silently loses the owner link. Breaks GC/owner-based cascade for ALL namespaced CRs (cluster-scoped CRs are unaffected). Live-reproduced via kubectl create (not just apply) against a namespaced CRD instance with ownerReferences in this session's Lima VM run.

Fix direction: apply the same save-owner-refs-before / restore-after pattern that stamp_cr_fields already uses, to the namespace-setting ObjectMeta round-trip in create_cr_namespaced — or reorder so stamp_cr_fields (which already does the safe round-trip) is the ONLY place ObjectMeta gets round-tripped, and fold the namespace assignment into it or do it via a direct JSON field set (obj["metadata"]["namespace"] = ...) instead of an ObjectMeta round-trip.

Needs its own regression test mirroring create_cr_via_api_preserves_owner_references but calling create_cr_namespaced instead of create_cr.

